  Licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.0 or later. See LICENSE.

  Cleartext HTTP is permitted.

  This is a deliberate decision, not an oversight. A very large share of real M3U
  playlists and Xtream panels are served over plain HTTP, and Vibrato connects only to
  hosts the user configured themselves (docs/FREEZE.md §3: direct client-to-provider, no
  proxy, no intermediary). Blocking cleartext would make the app unusable with most of
  the sources its users actually have, while protecting nothing the project controls.

  The trade-off is real and users should understand it: traffic to an http:// source,
  including any credentials embedded in that URL by the provider, is visible to the
  network. That is a property of the source the user chose, not of Vibrato.

  System CA certificates only. User-installed CAs are deliberately NOT trusted, so a
  device with a rogue or corporate root cannot silently intercept HTTPS sources.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
